# Translations

User-visible text goes through `t("some.id")` — never a literal in a template or a JS string.

`locales/en.json` is the source of truth. Adding, renaming or removing a key there means doing
the same in all 19 other `locales/*.json`, actually translated, in the same key order. A key
that exists only in English is an unfinished change, not a fallback.

Editing an English string means retranslating it in all 19 too. Nothing catches that — the
build only warns about *missing* keys, never stale ones, and `scripts/compile-i18n.mjs` fills
a missing key in from English rather than failing. So verify before calling the work done:

```bash
npm run i18n   # any "<code>: N keys falling back to English" line means it is not done
```

- `{placeholder}` names stay identical across locales.
- `*_one` / `*_other` plural pairs are added and removed together.
- `meta.language_name` is written in its own language.
- `src/lib/i18n-data.js` is generated — never edit it.
